  6. How reported_health and suggested_health are handled
  
  suggested_health is computed server-side at creation by compute_suggested_health(program) in app/domain/health.py. It calls program_health_state (5-state) and maps: off_track → off_track, at_risk | needs_attention → at_risk,
  on_track | inactive → on_track. It is stored once and never recomputed on edit — patching a report does not change suggested_health.

  reported_health is TPM-declared. Accepted on create and on edit. Validated against STATUS_REPORT_HEALTHS = ("on_track", "at_risk", "off_track") in the UI handler. Schema uses Literal["on_track", "at_risk", "off_track"]. Database
  CheckConstraint enforces it at the storage layer.

  ---
  7. Health values — only on_track / at_risk / off_track?
  
  Yes, enforced at three layers:
  1. Pydantic schema: Literal["on_track", "at_risk", "off_track"] on StatusReportCreate.reported_health
  2. UI validation: reported_health not in STATUS_REPORT_HEALTHS gate before creation
  3. DB CheckConstraints on both reported_health and suggested_health

  ---
  8. needs_attention not used as a health value?
  
  Confirmed — never stored. needs_attention appears in three places, all correct:
  - HEALTH_STATES (the 5-state computed health tuple for the program list filter — unrelated to reports)
  - compute_suggested_health() maps it to "at_risk" before storage
  - One test function name (test_suggested_health_at_risk_for_needs_attention) — tests that the mapping works correctly

  needs_attention cannot appear in any StatusReport row because the CheckConstraint excludes it.

